            },
            "createdOn": 1762193878,
            "author": {
                "name": "Brad Bishop",
                "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                "username": "bradbishop"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I think that should be enough time."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm a bit torn on this one. I get the frustration but also, with this change we're basically going to silently fail. If this feature is enabled, the expectation is the system better serve up the configuration and if it doesn't, we want to know. Should the vanilla default be to not enable this \"use-json\" PACKAGECONFIG?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": ">  If this feature is enabled, the expectation is the system better serve up the configuration\n\nSomeone could just as easily want to use this feature flag to support two systems, one with leds and one without, in a single flash image and their expectation would be that the service does something sane on the system without any configuration.\n\n> and if it doesn't, we want to know.\n\nWell, you do know, because its in the journal when this happens.  Can you say more about what you want to happen?  Maybe I can do something about it.\n\n> Should the vanilla default be to not enable this \"use-json\" PACKAGECONFIG?\n\nYes probably, but I think we'd want a proper entity-manager schema for led groups before we did that."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "> Well, you do know, because its in the journal when this happens. Can you say more about what you want to happen? Maybe I can do something about it.\n\nA reported error would be great, maybe just a warning error level as a compromise to address your use case of 2 systems being supported by a single flash image."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "> maybe just a warning error level\n\ndone.\n\n> A reported error would be great\n\nWhat is the mechanism for doing this?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Geissler",
                        "email": "geissonator@yahoo.com",
                        "username": "geissonator"
                    },
                    "message": "> What is the mechanism for doing this?\n\nSomething like https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-state-manager/blob/master/systemd_target_signal.cpp#L53"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "> Something like https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-state-manager/blob/master/systemd_target_signal.cpp#L53\n\nOk, thanks for the reference.  IMO that is leaving the realm of sane behavior on the platform without leds, since we'd be reporting an error external to the BMC."
                }
            ],
